    CARRIERCAM after ruleArgBody=0x100652cd0   (want == resultBody)
    BODY  argument   node=0x100657540  groupBody=0x100652960

⚠⚠ READ THOSE FOUR LINES IN ORDER, BECAUSE THEY EXONERATE THE OBVIOUS SUSPECT.
THE BIND WORKED. ruleArg's body went 0x100652960 -> 0x100652cd0, which is
exactly resultBody. The flip's other half does precisely what it was designed to
do, on the node it was handed.

THE CALLEE THEN READS A DIFFERENT NODE. The bind wrote node 0x100651780. The
callee's `argument` is node 0x100657540 -- and it carries 0x100652960, which is
ruleArg's body BEFORE the bind. So the read node is a copy or a sibling minted
from ruleArg ahead of the assignment, and the write never reaches it.

AND ONE UNWRAP OF THE CARRIER YIELDS NOTHING:

    ERROR unary * on argument -- it holds no group
    value bare      = argument      (bear-trap 26: no data, so the tag comes back)
    value unwrapped = 0

So it is not even a wrapper POINTING at the subject. Under legacy the else-arm
sets `ruleArg->setGroup(result)` and the node carries a group; under the flip
that arm is skipped and the body adoption lands elsewhere, so the read node ends
up with NEITHER. That is why the carrier is empty rather than merely indirect.

